  "before_interfaces": {
    "def-latent-variable-joint-and-observed-data-factorization": [
      "## Definition\n\nLet $(\\Theta,\\mathcal T)$, $(\\mathsf X,\\mathcal A)$, and\n$(\\mathsf Z,\\mathcal C)$ be measurable spaces (sets equipped with\nsigma-algebras; [[def-measurable-space]]). Let $\\nu$ and $\\mu$ be sigma-finite\nreference measures on the observation and latent spaces; each has a countable\nmeasurable cover by sets of finite measure\n([[def-finite-sigma-finite-and-semifinite-measures]]). The finite product\nsigma-algebra is generated by measurable rectangles\n([[def-product-sigma-algebra-and-finite-product-sigma-algebras]]). The product\nreference measure $\\nu\\otimes\\mu$ is the one supplied by\n[[thm-sigma-finite-product-measure-exists-is-rectangular-and-is-unique]].\n\nA **jointly measurable latent-variable density** is a specified function\n$$ p:\\Theta\\times\\mathsf X\\times\\mathsf Z\\longrightarrow[0,\\infty] $$\nmeasurable for $\\mathcal T\\otimes\\mathcal A\\otimes\\mathcal C$ and normalized\non every parameter fibre:\n$$ \\int_{\\mathsf X\\times\\mathsf Z}p(\\theta,x,z)\\, (\\nu\\otimes\\mu)(d(x,z))=1\\qquad(\\theta\\in\\Theta). $$\nHere $x$ is the observed coordinate and $z$ the latent coordinate. The\nobserved marginal density is\n$$ m(\\theta,x):=\\int_{\\mathsf Z}p(\\theta,x,z)\\,\\mu(dz). $$\nThe valid-fibre set is\n$V:=\\{(\\theta,x):0<m(\\theta,x)<\\infty\\}$. Only on $V$ define the normalized\nlatent-fibre density\n$$ q(z\\mid\\theta,x):=\\frac{p(\\theta,x,z)}{m(\\theta,x)}. $$\nThis definition requires a single jointly measurable density as an input.\n[[def-likelihood-density]] supplies neither a sigma-algebra on $\\Theta$ nor\njoint parameter measurability from separately specified likelihood sections;\nthe joint measurability above is an additional hypothesis. Because $p$ is\nalready supplied as one joint function, this definition uses no choice axiom.\nIt does not assert that AC can turn separately specified pointwise likelihood\nversions into a jointly measurable version. No quotient or pointwise\nconditional density is asserted on a zero or infinite marginal fibre."
    ],
    "thm-kullback-leibler-is-nonnegative": [
      "## Statement\n\nAssume AC and use the directional convention of\n[[def-kullback-leibler-divergence]]. For probability measures $P,Q$ on one\nmeasurable space, $D(P\\|Q)\\ge0$, and $D(P\\|Q)=0$ if and only if $P=Q$.\n\nThere is also the following normalized-density identity. Let\n$(\\mathsf X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ be a sigma-finite measure space: it is a set\nwith a sigma-algebra and measure ([[def-measure-space]]), and there is a\ncountable measurable cover by sets of finite $\\mu$-measure\n([[def-finite-sigma-finite-and-semifinite-measures]]). Let\n$\\gamma:\\mathsf X\\to[0,\\infty)$ be a finite-valued measurable function with\n$Z:=\\int\\gamma\\,d\\mu\\in(0,\\infty)$, and put $\\pi=\\gamma/Z$. Let\n$q:\\mathsf X\\to[0,\\infty)$ be a finite-valued measurable probability density,\nso $\\int q\\,d\\mu=1$, and suppose $q=0$ $\\mu$-almost everywhere on\n$\\{\\gamma=0\\}$. Assume\n$$ \\int q\\bigl(|\\log\\gamma|+|\\log q|\\bigr)\\,d\\mu<\\infty, $$\nwhere each term with $q=0$ is defined to be zero. Then\n$$ \\log Z =\\int q(\\log\\gamma-\\log q)\\,d\\mu +D(q\\,\\mu\\|\\pi\\,\\mu). $$\nIn particular, the first integral is at most $\\log Z$. This identity concerns\nnormalized densities only; it does not assert posterior existence for an\nunspecified statistical model."
    ],
    "alg-gradient-descent": [
      "## Definition\n\nLet $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^d$ be open and let $f:U\\to\\mathbb R$ be continuously\nFréchet differentiable. The gradient $\\nabla f(x)$ is the vector of partial\nderivatives in the Euclidean coordinates, with the convention of\n[[def-jacobian-matrix-and-gradient]]; differentiability is the Fréchet\ncondition of [[def-real-frechet-derivative-on-real-and-complex-matrix-spaces]].\n\nGiven an initial point $x_0\\in U$, a stopping rule, and either a positive\nfixed-step sequence $(t_k)$ or backtracking parameters\n$\\alpha\\in(0,1)$ and $\\beta\\in(0,1)$, repeat the following step at $x_k$:\n\n1. Compute $g_k:=\\nabla f(x_k)$. If $g_k=0$, stop and return $x_k$.\n2. Set the descent direction $p_k:=-g_k$.\n3. In fixed-step mode, use the supplied $t_k>0$. In backtracking mode,\n   start with $t=1$ and replace $t$ by $\\beta t$ until\n   $x_k+tp_k\\in U$ and\n   $f(x_k+tp_k)\\le f(x_k)+\\alpha t\\,\\langle g_k,p_k\\rangle$.\n4. Set $x_{k+1}:=x_k+t p_k$. If a fixed-step candidate leaves $U$, stop\n   with domain failure.\n\nThe procedure asserts no convergence or global-minimization property for an\narbitrary objective. Its backtracking branch is well-defined at every\nnonstationary iterate by\n[[lem-armijo-backtracking-terminates-for-strict-descent-directions]]."
    ],
    "alg-damped-newton": [
      "## Definition\n\nLet $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^d$ be open and let $f:U\\to\\mathbb R$ be twice\ncontinuously Fréchet differentiable. Write $g(x)=\\nabla f(x)$ and\n$H(x)=\\nabla^2 f(x)$ using [[def-jacobian-matrix-and-gradient]] and\n[[def-hessian-and-euclidean-critical-point]]. The Hessian is symmetric by\n[[cor-hessian-is-symmetric]].\n\nGiven $x_0\\in U$, stopping rules, and line-search parameters\n$\\alpha\\in(0,1/2)$, $\\beta\\in(0,1)$, repeat at $x_k$:\n\n1. If $g(x_k)=0$, stop and return $x_k$.\n2. Require $H(x_k)$ to be positive definite. Factor\n   $H(x_k)=L_kL_k^{T}$ by Cholesky, solve\n   $L_kw=-g(x_k)$ and $L_k^{T}p_k=w$.\n   If the Hessian is not positive definite, stop without a Newton update.\n3. Start with $t=1$ and replace $t$ by $\\beta t$ until\n   $x_k+t p_k\\in U$ and\n   $f(x_k+t p_k)\\le f(x_k)+\\alpha t\\,\\langle g(x_k),p_k\\rangle$.\n4. Set $x_{k+1}:=x_k+t p_k$ and apply the caller's stopping rule.\n\nThe Cholesky factor and Newton solve are well-defined when the Hessian is\npositive definite by\n[[thm-cholesky-factorisation-exists-iff-hermitian-positive-definite-and-is-unique]].\nThe backtracking branch terminates at a nonstationary iterate by\n[[lem-armijo-backtracking-terminates-for-strict-descent-directions]]. This\nprocedure makes no global-optimality claim."
    ],
    "thm-observed-density-is-latent-marginal-under-tonelli": [
      "## Statement\n\nAssume the hypotheses of\n[[def-latent-variable-joint-and-observed-data-factorization]]. Then\n$m(\\theta,x)=\\int p(\\theta,x,z)\\,\\mu(dz)$ is jointly measurable in\n$(\\theta,x)$. For each fixed $\\theta$, it is an observed-data probability\ndensity: it is $\\mathcal A$-measurable and\n$$ \\int_{\\mathsf X}m(\\theta,x)\\,\\nu(dx)=1. $$\nConsequently $m(\\theta,x)<\\infty$ for $\\nu$-almost every $x$. On every valid\nfibre $0<m(\\theta,x)<\\infty$, $q(z\\mid\\theta,x)=p(\\theta,x,z)/m(\\theta,x)$ is\na measurable $\\mu$-density of total mass one. Finally, if $h:\\Theta\\times\\mathsf X\\times\\mathsf Z\\to\\mathbb C$ is product\nmeasurable and\n$\\int |h(\\theta,x,z)|p(\\theta,x,z)\\,d(\\nu\\otimes\\mu)<\\infty$ for a fixed\n$\\theta$, Fubini permits exchanging the latent and observed integrals of\n$hp$. No exchange is claimed without this absolute integrability."
    ],
    "lem-armijo-backtracking-terminates-for-strict-descent-directions": [
      "## Statement\n\nFor either backtracking procedure in [[alg-gradient-descent]] and\n[[alg-damped-newton]], suppose the objective is differentiable at a point\n$x$ of an open domain and the current direction $p$ satisfies $Df(x)[p]<0$.\nIf the line search starts at $t=1$, uses a factor $\\beta\\in(0,1)$, and\naccepts when\n$$ f(x+tp)\\le f(x)+\\alpha t\\,Df(x)[p] $$\nfor a fixed $\\alpha\\in(0,1)$, it accepts after finitely many reductions.\nFor the Newton branch, assume the current Hessian is symmetric positive\ndefinite."
    ],
    "thm-gradient-descent-rate-under-strong-convexity-and-smoothness": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $f:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\mathbb R$ be continuously differentiable and have an\nattained minimum $f^*=f(x^*)$. Say that $f$ is $L$-smooth when\n$\\|\\nabla f(x)-\\nabla f(y)\\|\\le L\\|x-y\\|$ for all $x,y$, and\n$\\mu$-strongly convex when\n$$ f(y)\\ge f(x)+\\langle\\nabla f(x),y-x\\rangle +\\frac{\\mu}{2}\\|y-x\\|^2 \\qquad(x,y\\in\\mathbb R^d). $$\nIf $0<\\mu\\le L$ and [[alg-gradient-descent]] uses the fixed step $1/L$, then\nthe initial gap is unchanged at $k=0$, and for every integer $k\\ge1$,\n$$ f(x_k)-f^*\\le \\left(1-\\frac{\\mu}{L}\\right)^k\\bigl(f(x_0)-f^*\\bigr). $$\nFor $\\mu=L$, the factor is zero only for the stated $k\\ge1$ range; no\n$0^0$ convention is used."
    ],
    "thm-newton-local-quadratic-convergence-under-lipschitz-hessian": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $d\\ge1$, let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^d$ be open, and let\n$f:U\\to\\mathbb R$ be $C^2$.\nUse the Hessian convention of [[def-hessian-and-euclidean-critical-point]].\nSuppose $x^*\\in U$ is an interior critical point,\n$H(x^*):=\\nabla^2f(x^*)$\nis positive definite, and for some $R>0$, $K\\ge0$ the ball $B(x^*,R)$ lies\nin $U$ and\n$$ \\|H(x)-H(y)\\|_{\\rm op}\\le K\\|x-y\\| \\qquad(x,y\\in B(x^*,R)). $$\nFix the Armijo parameters $0<\\alpha<1/2$ and $0<\\beta<1$ in\n[[alg-damped-newton]]. There are $\\rho>0$ and $C<\\infty$ such that every\nstart $x_0\\in B(x^*,\\rho)$ stays in this ball, the algorithm accepts the full\nstep $t_k=1$ at each nonstationary iterate, and\n$$ \\|x_{k+1}-x^*\\|\\le C\\|x_k-x^*\\|^2. $$\nThis is a local statement; it supplies no global optimum or convergence\nclaim for a nonconvex objective."
    ],
